    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n24337" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δηλονότι</span></b> , i.e. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δῆλόν
			[ἐστιν] ὅτι</span> (cf. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δῆλος</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					11.3
				</span></i>
			), used adverbially, <i>clearly, manifestly,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Cri.</u>
				53a
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Grg.</u>
				487d
			</span></i>, etc.; once in <i>NT,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				<u>1 Ep.Cor.</u>
				15.27
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> freq.
			epexegetically, <i>that is to say, namely,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Smp.</u>
				199a
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>Cyr.</u>
				5.4.6
			</span></i>, etc.: in Gramm. the common form for introducing an
			explanation, Sch.<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Ach.</u>
				11
			</span></i>, etc.</div>
